data\torso-stepped-README.md:39:| Lower post holes | X 65, Y 297, 317, 337 |
data\torso-stepped-README.md:61:| Case | Gusset | Displacement, mm | Peak, MPa | 99th percentile, MPa |
data\torso-stepped-README.md:65:| Tip-over, side | 3 mm aluminium | 1.8 | 297 | 100 |
data\torso-stepped-README.md:70:Normal use does not need the gusset. The tip-over does: without it, the 99th percentile is over yield.
data\torso-stepped-README.md:83:Bolts in the tip-over: 4.3 kN per plate over 5 bolts is about 860 N each. Bearing is 17 MPa on 10 mm PETG and 57 MPa on 3 mm aluminium. Friction on an M5 T-nut is only about 1 kN, assumed, and clamping through PETG relaxes, so printed plates are likely to slip onto the bolts.
data\torso-stepped-README.md:85:**Printed plates survive but do not protect the frame.** 10 mm PETG has about 9% of the in-plane stiffness of 3 mm aluminium, so the frame still takes nearly the no-gusset load. Estimated side displacement is about 5 mm against 1.8 mm. **Recommendation: 3 mm 6061-T6 in the same outline**, with a larger radius at the X 113, Y 335 corner, where the 297 MPa peak sits.
data\torso-stepped-README.md:106:A's 99th percentile sits at yield in the fall (145 MPa) with bonded ends. Real corner brackets will slip before that, so check them after a fall.
data\torso-stepped-README.md:114:| Lower posts, Y 155 to 353.62 | 4 | 198.62 | Cut from the 340 mm posts |
data\torso-stepped-README.md:120:| Step side rails, X ±103, Y 353.62 to 373.62 | 2 | 100 | Existing shelf side rails |
data\torso-stepped-README.md:127:New extrusion is 1024 mm. Total goes from 2460 to 3124 mm, about 332 g more, assuming 0.5 g/mm. Add 40 g for gussets plus 36 M5 T-nuts and bolts.
data\torso-stepped-README.md:133:| Compute shelf and Pi | No change. New rails at X ±65 keep the 110 mm span |
data\torso-stepped-README.md:140:The shell only has 8 to 17 mm to the frame at the back and sides, so 40 mm of crush was optimistic. Linear scaling from the 3 mm aluminium runs. Limit used: 99th percentile at 145 MPa yield.
data\torso-stepped-README.md:142:Back case: frame held on the rear rails of the upper box, 5.3 kN of lower body, 3.2 mm, 99th percentile 146 MPa. See `STEPPED_FALL_BACK_3MM.png`. Holding one face is harsher than a real landing on the whole shell.
data\torso-stepped-README.md:144:| Stop | Peak factor | Load | Side, 99th pct | Back, 99th pct | Holds? |
data\torso-stepped-README.md:149:| 30 mm | 1.5 | 8.8 kN | 133 MPa | 194 MPa | Side only |
data\torso-stepped-README.md:152:The frame needs about 30 mm of stop at the side and 40 mm at the back. A side fall lands on the arm first, which stands more than 100 mm proud of the frame, so 30 mm is reachable there if the printed arm parts crush. The back has nothing proud of it, so it needs a pad. Bolted joints will slip or tear before these stresses matter, so treat 2020 and its T-nuts as parts to inspect after any fall.
data\torso-stepped-README.md:167:The PDB and harness clips are not in the CAD, so those cannot be checked yet. Keep them off these slots. Pi ports at the rear also need plug room inside the 8 to 17 mm shell gap.
data\body-shell-manufacturing-README.md:28:| Across the back of the upper box | 6 to 8 mm | 10 to 17 mm |
data\body-shell-manufacturing-README.md:37:**Cross-thread consequence:** the arm thread's tip-over FEA assumes 40 mm of shell and link crush. The shell has 8 to 17 mm of gap to the
